You are supporting a process mining research prototype.
Use only the synthetic scenario information provided below.
Do not invent company-specific system names, product names, confidential logs, internal metrics, or real organizational details.
Return valid JSON only. Do not wrap it in Markdown.

Scenario ID: S06
Scenario title: Vendor Meeting and Improvement Action Follow-up
Initial workflow description:
The team meets with an equipment vendor to discuss improvement ideas. After the meeting, someone summarizes the discussion and follows up on action items.

Domain expert answers:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "Which roles from your organization typically attend the vendor meeting, and which specific role is responsible for producing the meeting summary afterward?",
      "answer": "The equipment engineer and meeting organizer attend from the organization, along with the vendor representative. The manager and action-item owner are also involved post-meeting. The meeting organizer is responsible for recording decisions and open questions during the meeting and for sharing the meeting summary afterward."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "What tool or system is currently used to record meeting notes and track action items—for example, email threads, a shared document, or a dedicated project management platform?",
      "answer": "The process uses a meeting notes system, an action-item tracker, and a shared document repository. A calendar system is also used. The specific product names of these tools are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "What does the meeting summary look like as a finished output—is it a free-text email, a structured template, or a formal document—and who receives it?",
      "answer": "The finished outputs are meeting minutes and an action-item list with owner and due date assignments. The specific format (free-text, template, or formal document) is not specified. The summary is shared by the meeting organizer, but the exact recipient list is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "How are action items assigned and prioritized after the meeting—is there a defined process for deciding who owns each item and when it must be completed?",
      "answer": "The manager is responsible for assigning action-item owners and due dates after the meeting. There is a defined decision point checking whether action items are clearly owned; if an owner is missing, that is treated as an exception. Prioritization criteria beyond owner and due date assignment are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "What happens when an action item is not completed by its agreed deadline—is there a formal escalation path, an automated reminder, or does it rely on someone manually following up?",
      "answer": "A missed follow-up deadline is listed as a recognized exception. Automation opportunities include automated action-item reminders and status tracking. However, a formal escalation path or manual follow-up procedure beyond that is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "How often do these vendor meetings occur, and what is the typical expected turnaround time for resolving the action items that come out of them?",
      "answer": "The frequency of vendor meetings and the expected turnaround time for resolving action items are not specified in the available information."
    }
  ]
}
